1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,856
These stations practise the school’s medical-device objectives. Begin
by identifying the device and patient’s immediate condition. The
lecture teaches observation, communication and escalation; it is not a
procedure manual.

2
00:00:14,856 --> 00:00:32,712
Do not remove or reposition a displaced device unless the immediate-
life-threat exception explicitly applies and the action is authorized
and taught. In this lab, do not insert, flush, reconnect, inflate or
test a device without direct instructor instruction.

3
00:00:32,712 --> 00:00:48,456
The instructor supplies a manikin or photo showing an enteral tube
that appears displaced. State what is visible, how the family
describes the change and what you cannot confirm. Ask when it was last
used and whether feeding or medicine was given afterward.

4
00:00:48,456 --> 00:01:04,992
Assess the patient’s condition and the site without testing or
advancing the tube. Report pain, vomiting, leakage, bleeding or
worsening perfusion; communicate that the device should not be used
until its status is confirmed by the appropriate clinician.

5
00:01:04,992 --> 00:01:21,288
Use the supplied chart or bag demonstration to report measured output
and the time interval. Separate a staff estimate from a recorded
measurement. Ask about the patient’s usual output, recent intake,
baseline cognition and the timing of new symptoms.

6
00:01:21,288 --> 00:01:37,968
A suspected blockage is not an invitation to manipulate the catheter.
The PCP’s scope does not automatically include urinary
catheterization, and school practice does not grant extra endorsement.
Escalate the patient’s condition through current service pathways.

7
00:01:37,968 --> 00:01:53,544
In an ostomy station, describe the appliance and visible skin without
removing the pouch. Ask what is normal for this person, when the
output changed, whether there is pain or leakage and whether food,
fluid or medication intake changed.

8
00:01:53,544 --> 00:02:11,184
The goal is to notice possible dehydration, skin injury or acute
abdominal change and share the facts with the receiving team. Do not
insert a finger, probe the stoma or attempt an appliance procedure
unless a specific current protocol and instructor supervision
authorize it.

9
00:02:11,184 --> 00:02:27,264
The integrated weekend scenario combines a device concern with a
second patient who may have a more immediate threat. Before dividing,
state the priority and the reason. Assign a named partner to each task
and set an exact trigger for a check-back.

10
00:02:27,264 --> 00:02:42,720
Do not allow the first patient to go unobserved while the second
patient is assessed. Use short status updates, repeat the priority
when the situation changes and request additional resources early if
one crew cannot safely cover both patients.

11
00:02:42,720 --> 00:03:02,616
For a patient with an enteral tube issue, include device type,
apparent displacement, last known use, site findings, abdominal
symptoms and whether the tube remained untouched. For a catheter
issue, include measured output and period, appearance, reported
blockage and systemic cues.

12
00:03:02,616 --> 00:03:28,320
For school practice, use the assigned SBAR or MIST format. Current
BCEHS A zero three instead specifies ATMIST AMBO for care transitions
and SBAR for consultation. State suspected sepsis or suspected
abdominal injury as a concern, not a proven diagnosis. Give the actual
findings, your request and the response, and verify accepted
responsibility.

13
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:57,264
These are fictional training data, not clinical thresholds. At ten
twenty, patient A is awake, oriented and speaking normally, with
abdominal pain rated six of ten. The caregiver now clarifies that the
tube was last used at nine and moved at ten ten. The external mark
changed and the dressing is moist. Internal position and injury remain
unconfirmed. Do not use or manipulate the tube.

14
00:03:57,264 --> 00:04:25,344
Patient B has new confusion, rapid breathing, low pressure and fever;
the collection record shows thirty millilitres over six hours.
Prioritize the immediate breathing and perfusion threats, request
appropriate help and use the current pathway. Assign suitable care and
check-back triggers for both patients. Neither a catheter infection
nor sepsis is proven, and a device repair must not replace whole-
patient care.

15
00:04:25,344 --> 00:05:06,432
For B, rehearse this SBAR consultation: I need urgent clinical support
for a care-home patient with new confusion, rapid breathing and low
pressure. Staff report a normally interactive baseline; the collection
record shows thirty millilitres in six hours. At ten twenty,
respirations are thirty, pulse one eighteen, pressure eighty-six over
fifty-two, saturation ninety-two percent on room air and temperature
thirty-eight point six. I am concerned about systemic illness
including possible sepsis. Please advise the receiving plan and
additional resources while we continue indicated support.

16
00:05:06,432 --> 00:05:51,144
For patient A, give this school report using mechanism, illness, signs
and treatment: movement reportedly shifted the feeding tube at ten
ten; new abdominal pain and nausea, with a moist dressing and changed
external mark, but no confirmed internal injury. At ten twenty,
respirations twenty, pulse ninety-six, pressure one eighteen over
seventy-two, saturation ninety-seven percent on room air and pain six
of ten. We assessed the patient, protected the tube from use or
manipulation and are arranging appropriate evaluation with the
patient. This is a school report example, not a replacement for the
current service transfer format.

17
00:05:51,144 --> 00:06:25,272
For the documentation branch, the simulator supplies these later
events. At ten thirty, patient A has the same measured values and pain
score. The patient accepts assessment and conveyance. The tube is not
used, and no medicine is administered. Another qualified crew accepts
patient B at ten twenty-eight. Patient A departs at ten thirty-one and
the receiving clinician accepts this patient at ten forty-five. These
are additional fictional facts for this branch, not events you may
assume in another case.

18
00:06:25,272 --> 00:07:28,608
A concise A-only narrative could read: At ten twenty, a fifty-five-
year-old woman at home reports new abdominal pain and nausea.
Caregiver reports the feeding tube was last used at nine and shifted
at ten ten. Awake, oriented, speaking normally; respirations twenty,
pulse ninety-six, pressure one eighteen over seventy-two, room-air
saturation ninety-seven percent, pain six of ten. Dressing moist and
external mark changed; internal position not confirmed. Tube not used
or manipulated; assessment and conveyance accepted, no medicine given.
At ten thirty, measurements and pain unchanged. Departed ten thirty-
one; receiving clinician accepted at ten forty-five. Add the actual
identifiers, required assessment, medicines, allergies and responsible
names from the scenario; unavailable facts stay unavailable. Keep B in
a separate record.

19
00:07:28,608 --> 00:07:45,192
The school plan sets a five-minute completion target for a legal PCR
practice. Treat the time limit as a training constraint, not
permission to omit required information or a claim about the law. Use
only the school rubric and authorized practice environment.

20
00:07:45,192 --> 00:08:07,992
Build a truthful timeline first. Then record the patient’s baseline,
device status, objective findings, authorized actions and response.
Include names or roles, consultation and handover. Each patient needs
a separate record; never merge two patients into one narrative or
invent a value to meet the timer.

21
00:08:07,992 --> 00:08:24,264
At the end of the timed drill, compare the PCR with the scenario
record and team observations. Correct chronology, source attribution,
device details and patient response. Separate “not assessed” from
“normal” and estimate from measurement.

22
00:08:24,264 --> 00:08:39,672
A legally and clinically useful record reflects what happened rather
than what should have happened. If a safety event or medication error
occurred, follow the actual service reporting and amendment process
instead of silently editing the narrative.

23
00:08:39,672 --> 00:08:54,600
The instructor will not lead the review. Ask the seven questions in
order, using the patient story and observable evidence. If your answer
relies on hindsight, state what information was available at the time
and what was not.

24
00:08:54,600 --> 00:09:11,256
The final question should produce one observable action for the next
run: for example, state the source and time for each device-output
figure, or assign the second patient a reassessment interval. The
instructor may then calibrate the target against the rubric.

25
00:09:11,256 --> 00:09:26,520
End each station by summarizing the patient’s concern, relevant device
change, main findings, actions, response and next concern. Use neutral
language and preserve the person’s dignity during examination and
movement.

26
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:44,256
This week practises NG/PEG, urinary-catheter and ostomy scenarios. The
purpose is recognition, reasoning, communication and documentation
under supervision—not independent device manipulation or competency
sign-off from an online lesson.
